{"_id":"68a7360f8ec3d8323af1314e","nodeId":"uqfeiQ9K--QkGNwks4kjk","roadmapSlug":"computer-science","createdAt":"2025-08-21T15:06:54.940Z","description":"# CAP Theorem\n\nCAP is an acronym for Consistency, Availability, and Partition Tolerance. According to the CAP theorem, any distributed system can only guarantee two of the three properties at any time. You can't guarantee all three properties at once.","resources":[{"type":"article","title":"What is CAP Theorem?","url":"https://www.bmc.com/blogs/cap-theorem/","_id":"6a7f4a1dcb44947debcf3bb4"},{"type":"article","title":"CAP Theorem - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CAP_theorem","_id":"6a7f4a1dcb44947debcf3bb5"},{"type":"article","title":"An Illustrated Proof of the CAP Theorem","url":"https://mwhittaker.github.io/blog/an_illustrated_proof_of_the_cap_theorem/","_id":"6a7f4a1dcb44947debcf3bb6"},{"type":"article","title":"CAP Theorem and its applications in NoSQL Databases","url":"https://www.ibm.com/uk-en/cloud/learn/cap-theorem","_id":"6a7f4a1dcb44947debcf3bb7"},{"type":"video","title":"What is CAP Theorem?","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_RbsFXWRZ10","_id":"6a7f4a1dcb44947debcf3bb8"}],"updatedAt":"2026-08-14T17:02:21.077Z","lessonPacks":[],"paidResources":[],"contribution":"https://github.com/kamranahmedse/developer-roadmap/tree/master/roadmaps/computer-science/content/uqfeiQ9K--QkGNwks4kjk.md"}